Insights: Shows that Retrieval-Augmented Generation augmented with OPORD outline schemas can substantially improve accuracy, transparency, and usability of LLM-based analysis for military operations orders. \textbar\textbar{} TL;DR: This presentation demonstrates a schema-aware RAG approach that enables high-accuracy question answering and task-based evaluation of OPORDs, reducing cognitive load on Army planners while preserving human oversight. \textbar\textbar{} Contributions: - Introduces a schema-aware RAG architecture that preserves OPORD hierarchical structure extracted from Microsoft Word XML (pp. 8--10). - Develops four LLM-based tools: OPORD RAG QA, Feature Extractor QA, OPORD Evaluator, and OPORD Task Tracker (pp. 6, 11, 14). - Demonstrates strong empirical performance across 240 evaluation questions, achieving 97.5\% overall accuracy (p. 16). - Applies METL-aligned grading (Trained/Needs Practice/Untrained) for automated OPORD section evaluation with human-in-the-loop validation (pp. 14--15). - Shows how schema-aware retrieval outperforms baseline RAG for structurally dependent OPORD queries (pp. 12--13). \textbar\textbar{} Challenges: - Evaluation dataset is limited to a single primary OPORD exercise (p. 17). - Limited testing with multiple planners and real training scenarios. - QA benchmark covers only a subset of annexes. - Further benchmarking against baseline and advanced RAG variants is needed.}
